<h1>Video 063 — Instance + static control flow (mixed)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: OOPs(Object Oriented Programming) Part-13 || instance, static control flow</p>
<p>ASR decode (Whisper): stringere axa / stringer a ox / string array acts = String[] args; testosterone / new test as hope = new Test(); SOP / espope = System.out.println; Yam / Yam 1 / M1 = method m1; Yam is = instance/static variable m; inistence = instance; Javiyam / JVMSI = JVM; neo / NEO = new; dead format / data format / Dead format = DateFormat; De-céralization / DC realization / D serialization = deserialization; Omani waste / our money waste = how many ways; tested / tester / study = Test t; for name = Class.forName; even instance / new instance = newInstance(); Inte x = int x; reference data = referenced.</p>
<h3>00:04 — Goal: mix instance + static control flow</h3>
<p>He takes a simple example where he wants to mix instance control flow and static control flow in one class, then decide the output order.</p>
<h3>00:15 — Board: class `Test` with both kinds of members</h3>
<p>He writes class Test. Members (top to bottom as built on the board):</p>
<ol><li>First instance block → prints &quot;First instance block&quot;</li></ol>
<ol><li>First static block → prints &quot;First static block&quot;</li></ol>
<ol><li>Constructor → prints &quot;Constructor&quot;</li></ol>
<ol><li>public static void main(String[] args) — creates t1, prints &quot;main&quot;, creates t2</li></ol>
<ol><li>Second static block → prints &quot;Second static block&quot;</li></ol>
<ol><li>Second instance block → prints &quot;Second instance block&quot;</li></ol>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    {
        System.out.println(&quot;First instance block&quot;);
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;First static block&quot;);
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Constructor&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
        Test t2 = new Test();
    }

    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Second static block&quot;);
    }

    {
        System.out.println(&quot;Second instance block&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>He stresses: instance block, static block, constructor — almost everything mixed simultaneously. Can you tell the output by default?</p>
<h3>02:30 — Walk the static control flow first</h3>
<p>Whenever the class is executed, static control flow runs first (already taught):</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members from top to bottom.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of static variable assignments and static blocks from top to bottom.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of main.</li></ol>
<p>How many static blocks? Two — first and second. So step 2 prints:</p>
<p>First static block
Second static block</p>
<p>That is “execution of static variable assignments and static blocks from top to bottom.”</p>
<p>Then execute main.</p>
<h3>03:36 — First `new Test()` → instance control flow</h3>
<pre class='code'>Inside main: Test t1 = new Test();</pre>
<p>Whenever we create an object:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of instance members from top to bottom.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of instance variable assignments and instance blocks from top to bottom.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execution of constructor.</li></ol>
<p>Two instance blocks (first, then second) → then constructor:</p>
<p>First instance block
Second instance block
Constructor</p>
<p>Object created. Then main prints &quot;main&quot;.</p>
<h3>04:22 — Second `new Test()` → instance control flow again</h3>
<pre class='code'>After main prints, he creates another object: Test t2 = new Test();</pre>
<p>Again: identify instance members, execute instance assignments/blocks top to bottom, then constructor:</p>
<p>First instance block
Second instance block
Constructor</p>
<p>Full default output:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    {
        System.out.println(&quot;First instance block&quot;);
    }
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;First static block&quot;);
    }
    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Constructor&quot;);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
        Test t2 = new Test();
    }
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Second static block&quot;);
    }
    {
        System.out.println(&quot;Second instance block&quot;);
    }
}
// java Test
// prints First static block
// prints Second static block
// prints First instance block
// prints Second instance block
// prints Constructor
// prints main
// prints First instance block
// prints Second instance block
// prints Constructor</pre>
<h3>05:06 — Critical observation: one-time vs per-object</h3>
<p>If instance control flow and static control flow are both considered, the order above is “nothing but life” — but a compulsory observation:</p>
<ul><li>Static control flow is a one-time activity (once per class load / class execution).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Instance control flow is not a one-time activity — for every object it runs again.</li></ul>
<p>For the first object you see the instance prints; for the second object you see them again.</p>
<h3>05:35 — Same example restated / notes dictation</h3>
<p>He rewrites / restates the same mixed Test (first instance block, static block, constructor, t1 / t2, second static block, second instance block) and drills the output again:</p>
<p>First static block
Second static block
First instance block
Second instance block
Constructor
main
First instance block
Second instance block
Constructor</p>
<p>You must decide in which order things execute. Clarity on mixed control flow is the goal of this part.</p>
<h3>08:00 — Next board example: `Initialization.java`</h3>
<p>He opens / recalls Initialization.java and writes it on the board so the order is easy to see.</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Initialization {
    private static String m1(String msg) {
        System.out.println(msg);
        return msg;
    }

    public Initialization() {
        m = m1(&quot;1&quot;);
    }

    {
        m = m1(&quot;2&quot;);
    }

    String m = m1(&quot;3&quot;);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object obj = new Initialization();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Helper m1: if you call it with a string argument, it prints that string and returns the same string. It is a static method.</p>
<h3>10:55 — Static steps for `Initialization`</h3>
<p>If we execute this class, first step: identification of static members from top to bottom.</p>
<p>How many static members? Only two:</p>
<ol><li>m1 method (static)</li></ol>
<ol><li>main method (static)</li></ol>
<p>Second step: execution of static variable assignments and static blocks — nothing here (no static variables / static blocks).</p>
<p>Third: execute main. Inside main we create an object → instance control flow starts.</p>
<h3>11:29 — Instance control flow for `Initialization`</h3>
<p>Whenever we create an object: identification of instance members from top to bottom.</p>
<p>Instance members:</p>
<ol><li>Constructor</li></ol>
<ol><li>Instance block (m = m1(&quot;2&quot;))</li></ol>
<ol><li>Instance variable String m = m1(&quot;3&quot;)</li></ol>
<p>Total: three instance members.</p>
<p>Next: execution of instance variable assignments and instance blocks from top to bottom.</p>
<ul><li>m is a String → JVM default `null` before assignments.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Instance block comes first in source order → m = m1(&quot;2&quot;) → prints `2`, returns &quot;2&quot;, assigns to m (null replaced with &quot;2&quot;).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Then instance variable assignment m = m1(&quot;3&quot;) → prints `3`, assigns &quot;3&quot; to m (replaces &quot;2&quot;).</li></ul>
<p>Last step: execute constructor → m = m1(&quot;1&quot;) → prints `1`, assigns &quot;1&quot;.</p>
<p>Output by default: `2`, `3`, `1`.</p>
<p>Order reason: instance block → instance variable assignment → constructor. Interview-style question: in which order will things execute?</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Initialization {
    private static String m1(String msg) {
        System.out.println(msg);
        return msg;
    }

    public Initialization() {
        m = m1(&quot;1&quot;);
    }

    {
        m = m1(&quot;2&quot;);
    }

    String m = m1(&quot;3&quot;);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object obj = new Initialization();
    }
}
// javac Initialization.java
// java Initialization
// prints 2
// prints 3
// prints 1</pre>
<p>He compiles and runs live: same output 2 3 1. Take the example and the output.</p>
<h3>15:39 — Pause / notes: mixed flows drive the order</h3>
<p>Instance control flow + static control flow mixed: you must drive the order yourself. Output for this demo is 2, 3, 1.</p>
<h3>17:02 — Next example: `Initialization2.java`</h3>
<p>Same idea, different mix. File: Initialization2.java.</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Initialization2 {
    private static String m1(String msg) {
        System.out.println(msg);
        return msg;
    }

    static String m = m1(&quot;1&quot;);

    {
        m = m1(&quot;2&quot;);
    }

    static {
        m = m1(&quot;3&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object obj = new Initialization2();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Members:</p>
<ul><li>Same static helper m1</li></ul>
<ul><li>Static variable static String m = m1(&quot;1&quot;)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Instance block m = m1(&quot;2&quot;)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Static block m = m1(&quot;3&quot;)</li></ul>
<ul><li>main creates new Initialization2()</li></ul>
<h3>19:53 — Static control flow for `Initialization2`</h3>
<p>First step: identification of static members from top to bottom.</p>
<p>Static members:</p>
<ol><li>m1 (static method)</li></ol>
<ol><li>static variable m (initially conceptually default null at identification)</li></ol>
<ol><li>static block</li></ol>
<ol><li>main</li></ol>
<p>Four static members.</p>
<p>Second step: execution of static variable assignments and static blocks from top to bottom.</p>
<ul><li>m = m1(&quot;1&quot;) → prints `1`, m becomes &quot;1&quot;</li></ul>
<ul><li>static block: m = m1(&quot;3&quot;) → prints `3`, m replaced with &quot;3&quot;</li></ul>
<p>Then execute main.</p>
<h3>21:10 — Object creation → only one instance member</h3>
<p>Within main, creating an object starts instance control flow.</p>
<p>Identification of instance members: only one — the instance block.</p>
<p>Execution: m = m1(&quot;2&quot;) → prints `2`, m becomes &quot;2&quot;.</p>
<p>Then execution of constructor: default constructor — does nothing visible.</p>
<p>Output by default: `1`, `3`, `2`.</p>
<pre class='code'>public class Initialization2 {
    private static String m1(String msg) {
        System.out.println(msg);
        return msg;
    }

    static String m = m1(&quot;1&quot;);

    {
        m = m1(&quot;2&quot;);
    }

    static {
        m = m1(&quot;3&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Object obj = new Initialization2();
    }
}
// javac Initialization2.java
// java Initialization2
// prints 1
// prints 3
// prints 2</pre>
<p>He compiles and runs: answer 1 3 2. Take example + output. Clarity on order of static vs instance execution.</p>
<h3>23:03 — Dictation wrap on mixed flows</h3>
<p>Static control flow and instance control flow — the order must be clear. He revisits the m1 / Initialization2 shape; completed: output 1, 3, 2.</p>
<h3>25:05 — Important conclusion: why static cannot touch instance directly</h3>
<p>Very important terminology / internal reason.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    int x = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}</pre>
<p>What is the answer? Do not overthink. Compiler error — covered several times:</p>
<p>From static area, we cannot access instance members directly.</p>
<p>Internal reason with control-flow language:</p>
<ol><li>Identification of static members — only main (here).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute static assignments / blocks — none.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Execute main → System.out.println(x).</li></ol>
<p>At that moment, JVM may not have identified instance member x. When is x identified? Whenever we create an object. Here we did not create an object → CE.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    int x = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}
// CE: non-static variable x cannot be referenced from a static context</pre>
<p>Exact message style he reads: non-static variable x cannot be referenced from a static context.</p>
<h3>27:53 — Fix: create object, then access via reference</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    int x = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();
        System.out.println(t.x);
    }
}
// java Test
// prints 10</pre>
<p>Valid: creating the object causes JVM to identify instance members; t.x is 10.</p>
<p>Note (dictation): From static area we cannot access instance members directly, because while executing static area, JVM may not identify instance members. Instance members are identified when we create an object.</p>
<p>Same CE demo restated:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    int x = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}
// CE: non-static variable x cannot be referenced from a static context</pre>
<p>With object — completely valid.</p>
<h3>31:04 — Interview bit: in how many ways can we get an object in Java?</h3>
<p>Very important for interviews. Wording he prefers: in how many ways can we get / create an object in Java?</p>
<p>There are standard ways — about 5 (books/faculties sometimes pad to 6–8 for number-counting; he wants standard ways applicable to most classes).</p>
<p>Keep writing: In how many ways can we create / get an object in Java?</p>
<h3>32:42 — Way 1: `new` operator</h3>
<p>Best-known / first approach:</p>
<pre class='code'>Test t = new Test();</pre>
<p>By using the `new` operator.</p>
<h3>33:13 — Way 2: `newInstance()` (Reflection API)</h3>
<p>By using `newInstance` method (Reflection API). Some people use this way.</p>
<p>Test t = (Test) Class.forName(&quot;Test&quot;).newInstance();</p>
<p>What happens:</p>
<ul><li>Class.forName(&quot;Test&quot;) — load Test class and create a Class class object</li></ul>
<ul><li>On that Class object, call newInstance()</li></ul>
<ul><li>newInstance() is present in class Class</li></ul>
<ul><li>Return type of newInstance() is Object → need type cast to Test</li></ul>
<p>He notes: difference between new and newInstance was covered earlier (operators chapter).</p>
<h3>34:36 — Way 3: factory methods</h3>
<p>By using a factory method.</p>
<p>Definition: by using the class name you call a method; that method returns the same class’s object, without the programmer writing new / newInstance at the call site. Programmer-friendly.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<p>Runtime r = Runtime.getRuntime();</p>
<p>Runtime.getRuntime() returns a Runtime object. Internally it may use new, but from the programmer’s point of view you get the object via a method → factory method.</p>
<p>Internationalization example:</p>
<p>DateFormat df = DateFormat.getInstance();</p>
<p>By class name, call getInstance → returns same-class (DateFormat) object → factory method.</p>
<h3>36:14 — Way 4: `clone()` method</h3>
<p>By using `clone`.</p>
<p>Assume one Test object; call clone to get an exact duplicate:</p>
<pre class='code'>Test t1 = new Test();
Test t2 = (Test) t1.clone();</pre>
<p>Exactly duplicate / clone object. Also a standard way to get an object.</p>
<h3>37:13 — Way 5: deserialization</h3>
<p>By using deserialization we can get an object.</p>
<p>Sketch (streams required — full serialization chapter later; do not worry now):</p>
<ul><li>To write an object → output stream</li></ul>
<ul><li>To read an object → input stream</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(&quot;abc.ser&quot;);
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(fis);
Dog d2 = (Dog) ois.readObject();</pre>
<p>Reading via readObject reconstitutes / gets the object (deserialization). He will explain properly in the serialization topic.</p>
<h3>39:09 — Standard five; ignore non-standard “extra” counts</h3>
<p>Standard ways:</p>
<ol><li>new operator</li></ol>
<ol><li>newInstance method</li></ol>
<ol><li>Factory method</li></ol>
<ol><li>clone method</li></ol>
<ol><li>Deserialization</li></ol>
<p>Some faculty / books add more options just for number-count. Examples that are not separate standard ways:</p>
<ul><li>Creating a String with a special style — not a general standard; applicable only to String.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Integer i = 10; — autoboxing; internally like Integer.valueOf(10), which is already the factory method idea — not a brand-new sixth concept.</li></ul>
<p>Serialization / new / newInstance / clone apply across classes. Discuss standard ways.</p>
<p>Answer for interview: 5 ways, with sample code expected.</p>
<h3>41:12 — Final dictation of the five ways</h3>
<p>Add answers clearly:</p>
<p>Sample board lines he revisits near the end:</p>
<p>Test t = new Test();

Test t = (Test) Class.forName(&quot;Test&quot;).newInstance();

Runtime r = Runtime.getRuntime();

DateFormat df = DateFormat.getInstance();
// (+ clone / deserialization as above)</p>
<h3>45:06 — Close</h3>
<p>These are the standard ways to create / get an object in Java. What you should be aware of for this session: mixed instance + static control flow order, the Initialization / Initialization2 output questions, why static cannot see instance without an object, and the five standard object-creation approaches.</p>
